			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">If you have another system around, install antivirus, update it and then remove infected hard disk to the 2nd machine and run full disk scan on it. Other than that the only thing else to try really is  safe mode... watch the list of files that loads and replace the last file you see before it reboots from a known good working xp machine (again you will have to install the hard drive into the known good xp machine). If all else fails and you are forced to reinstall, I would suggest a full repair instead (also depends on the actual problem which is undetermined). Put in you OS disk, boot, it will usually ask you if you would like to clean install, repair (this repair option is used to get to a command line and edit a config file for instance, not what we need), or cancel... choose clean install. Read the screens that follow carefully and pay attention to what options you have to choose from. Setup program will check the hard drive for existing versions of windows, finding your old one (usually in c:\winnt) it will ask again if you want to repair (this repair is the one you want, it checks all your main windows system files to see if they have been modified since original install, and if so it will copy the original file back restoring the main OS. Which usually fixes such boot problems but wont affect anything else)
